title: The Piedmont Klan Crisis
subtitle: A governor defies the will of the courts.
tags: event
new-page: true
view-if: NC_gub_winner = "rep" and NC_kkk_strength + NC_whiteL_strength >= 80
max-visits: 1
on-arrival: {!
    Q.NC_kkk_strength = (Q.NC_kkk_strength || 0) + 4;
    Q.kkk_strength += 1;
    Q.NC_freemen_rep -= 3;
    Q.NC_rural_dem += 1;
!}
face-image: img/George_Washington_Kirk.jpg

= The Piedmont Klan Crisis

Reports from North Carolina's Piedmont describe a campaign of murder and intimidation against <span style="color: #c00000;">**Republican**</span> officials and their supporters. In one county, a masked band seized a Black town commissioner from his home and hanged him in the courthouse square. A warning left on the body threatened Black and white <span style="color: #c00000;">**Republicans**</span> alike.

Several weeks later, a <span style="color: #c00000;">**Republican**</span> state senator was lured into a courthouse basement during a <span style="color: #0015bc;">**Democratic**</span> nominating convention and stabbed to death. The coroner's inquest returned a finding of death at the hands of persons unknown. The local sheriff, himself suspected of Klan membership, helped conduct the inquiry.

The governor has repeatedly petitioned for authority to suppress the violence. The legislature has now granted him additional militia powers, though it struck out a provision permitting the suspension of habeas corpus. Washington has issued no orders for military deployment against the Klan.

The governor has acted without waiting for further instructions. He has raised a militia in the western mountains, where Unionist sentiment remains strongest, and placed a former Union raider in command. Within days, the force occupied two county seats and arrested a number of suspected Klansmen, including a former congressman and both county sheriffs. Klan activity in the affected counties has since fallen sharply.

The violent crackdown have provoked a constitutional dispute as the state's chief justice has issued a writ of habeas corpus demanding that the prisoners be brought before the court. The governor has refused to comply, despite lacking statutory authority to suspend the writ. He has now appealed to Washington for support.

[? if NC_occupation_posture >= 1 : An estimated [+ NC_garrison_troops +] thousand federal troops are stationed in North Carolina. Their presence may prove decisive if the dispute escalates further. ?][? if NC_occupation_posture < 1 : No federal troops are stationed in North Carolina, leaving the state government to confront the crisis on its own. ?]

- @back_governor: Back the governor and permit the military tribunal to proceed
- @honor_writ: Order the governor to honor the writ and submit the prisoners to the courts

@back_governor
on-arrival: {!
    let troops = Q.NC_garrison_troops || 0;
    Q.NC_kkk_strength = Math.max(0, (Q.NC_kkk_strength || 0) - Math.min(28, 14 + troops * 0.8));
    Q.NC_freemen_rep += 3;
    Q.NC_rural_dem += 3;
    Q.NC_middle_class_dem += 2;
    Q.radical_strength += 2;
    Q.liberal_dissent += 4;
    Q.conservative_dissent += 2;
    Q.dem_relation -= 5;
!}

Washington has endorsed the governor's actions. The military tribunal retains custody of the prisoners, and Klan operations in the two counties have largely ceased. The decision has drawn immediate criticism from <span style="color: #DAA520;">**Liberal Republicans**</span>, who warn that the administration has sanctioned imprisonment without trial. <span style="color: #0015bc;">**Democratic**</span> newspapers describe the affair as an assault on constitutional government.

@honor_writ
on-arrival: {!
    Q.NC_kkk_strength = Math.max(0, (Q.NC_kkk_strength || 0) - 6);
    Q.NC_freemen_rep -= 2;
    Q.NC_rural_dem += 1;
    Q.liberal_dissent = Math.max(0, (Q.liberal_dissent || 0) - 2);
    Q.dem_relation += 2;
!}

The administration has instructed the governor to comply with the writ. The prisoners are brought before the courts, but the prosecutions quickly falter. Witnesses refuse to testify in the presence of suspected Klansmen, and local juries decline to convict their neighbors. Within weeks, the prisoners have been released.

Klan activity reduces but mostly resumes in the surrounding counties. Critics of the decision argue that the courts have proved unable to protect those who testify against the organization.